   "book": "îmbrăcăminte",
   "spoken": "haine",
   "gloss": "clothes",
   "note": "The chapter's headword 'îmbrăcăminte' is the formal, collective word — you see it on shop signs and forms. In speech everyone says 'haine' for clothes ('unde sunt hainele?'). 'O haină' on its own colloquially means a coat or jacket. Learn haine first; keep îmbrăcăminte for reading.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "type": "missing_from_book",
   "spoken": "roșu, verde, maro, gri",
   "gloss": "red, green, brown, grey",
   "note": "The book gives only alb, negru, albastru, galben. You will want at least red and green immediately. Note the agreement quirks: roșu / roșie / roșii behaves like negru; verde has just verde / verzi; maro and gri don't change at all (o rochie maro, niște pantaloni gri).",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "type": "written_vs_spoken",
   "book": "paltonul, puloverul, fularul, tricoul, impermeabilul",
   "spoken": "paltonu', puloveru', fularu', tricou', impermeabilu'",
   "gloss": "the coat, the sweater, the scarf, the t-shirt, the raincoat",
   "note": "This is the chapter that formally teaches the -ul 'the' ending — and it is exactly the ending spoken Romanian clips. You will learn 'paltonul' and hear 'paltonu''. Both are the same word; the full -l is the careful/written form. Feminine -a stays (cămașa, rochia).",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "type": "register_scale",
   "book": "rău",
   "spoken": "urât (for clothes) / nu-mi place",
   "gloss": "bad → ugly / I don't like it",
   "note": "'Rău' is the book's word for bad, but about a person it leans toward mean/evil, and about clothes it sounds odd. A garment you dislike is 'urât' (ugly), or you just say 'nu-mi place' (I don't like it). Keep rău as the opposite of bun in the abstract; reach for urât in front of a wardrobe.",
   "confidence": "check"
  }
 ],
